00:41:38.920 --> 00:41:41.950
strong enough to discourage them
from continuing. And we had to

00:41:41.950 --> 00:41:45.580
talk to them about staying in
their programs. And thus the

00:41:45.580 --> 00:41:49.810
idea comes forward. If we're
going to face the inevitability

00:41:49.810 --> 00:41:52.960
of a racist experience, perhaps
we should be armed for racist

00:41:52.960 --> 00:41:55.780
experiences. And that doesn't
mean that all of you or all of

00:41:55.780 --> 00:41:59.890
us are racist, simply that the
reaction, the idea of

00:41:59.890 --> 00:42:04.480
resistance, and resilience is
one that we must live with. When

00:42:04.480 --> 00:42:07.360
I moved to BC, with my new
medical degree, and I went to

00:42:07.360 --> 00:42:10.390
St. Paul's hospital, I went into
the doctor's lounge, I was very

00:42:10.390 --> 00:42:14.140
proud, I looked exactly like
this. within five minutes, the

00:42:14.140 --> 00:42:17.230
security guard came up to me.
And he said, I'm I'm sorry, sir,

00:42:17.230 --> 00:42:19.570
one of the doctors has reported
that there's an Indian in the

00:42:19.570 --> 00:42:23.770
doctors lounge. And I laughed,
and I said, Yeah, you better get

00:42:23.770 --> 00:42:27.940
used to it. Part of my work is
to work with Aboriginal

00:42:27.940 --> 00:42:31.030
populations. One day, we had a
chief who was fighting for the

00:42:31.030 --> 00:42:34.600
return of an infant who had
died. The chief coroner said by

00:42:34.600 --> 00:42:38.560
law, I'm allowed to keep this
infant body for as long as I see

00:42:38.560 --> 00:42:42.070
fit. And I can remove parts of
that body and keep them in order

00:42:42.070 --> 00:42:45.070
to determine a cause of death.
And the Chiefs slammed his fist

00:42:45.430 --> 00:42:48.250
on the ground or on the table
and said, We know you think

00:42:48.250 --> 00:42:54.670
you're in charge. But we think
that's our baby. So to get where

00:42:54.670 --> 00:42:59.380
we need to go, we may need to
fly in a different way. I showed

00:42:59.380 --> 00:43:02.680
this to an elder. And she said,
Oh, yeah, there's male eagle

00:43:02.680 --> 00:43:06.400
flies up down when it is having
sex. And I said, Oh, that's not

00:43:06.400 --> 00:43:10.570
quite what I wanted to say. And
she said, Yeah, that's a sexy

00:43:10.570 --> 00:43:11.380
Eagle. Thank you.

00:43:23.970 --> 00:43:28.230
Rebecca Thomas: Kwe, ni'n
teluisi Rebecca. Hi, my name is

00:43:28.230 --> 00:43:29.760
Rebecca. Shall we?

00:43:31.550 --> 00:43:34.640
The language you speak
fundamentally shapes your

00:43:34.640 --> 00:43:37.580
worldview and everything that
goes along with it. And this

00:43:37.580 --> 00:43:41.450
concept is specifically
amplified. When it comes to

00:43:41.510 --> 00:43:45.590
indigenous languages. indigenous
language weaves its way through

00:43:45.590 --> 00:43:49.790
every aspect of who we are as
people from how we interact with

00:43:49.790 --> 00:43:52.850
the physical world, how we build
our communities and how we carry

00:43:52.850 --> 00:43:57.200
our culture forward. And with
that comes a unique perspective.

00:43:57.680 --> 00:44:00.320
And it's that perspective that
I'm going to try to create for

00:44:00.320 --> 00:44:03.650
you so that when I introduce the
concept of etuaptmumk, of

00:44:03.680 --> 00:44:06.860
two-eyed seeing, you'll
understand why it's so

00:44:06.860 --> 00:44:11.450
important. So to begin, a study
published in psychological

00:44:11.450 --> 00:44:16.340
sciences called two languages,
two minds broke down grammatical

00:44:16.340 --> 00:44:21.350
tools that situate actions in
time, they showed photographs to

00:44:21.590 --> 00:44:24.860
English and German speakers and
asked them to describe what was

00:44:24.860 --> 00:44:28.460
going on in them. So for
example, they showed a woman

00:44:28.460 --> 00:44:32.330
walking in a parking lot.
English speakers said, this is a

00:44:32.330 --> 00:44:35.930
woman walking, whereas German
speaker said this is a woman

00:44:35.930 --> 00:44:39.410
walking to her car, they showed
another photograph a man riding

00:44:39.410 --> 00:44:43.880
his bicycle. Once again, English
speakers honed in on the action

00:44:43.880 --> 00:44:46.850
and said this is a man riding
his bicycle, whereas German

00:44:46.850 --> 00:44:50.120
speakers needed to add a
destination to complete their

00:44:50.120 --> 00:44:53.120
context, even though that
destination wasn't featured in

00:44:53.120 --> 00:44:56.450
the photograph. This is a man
riding his bicycle to the

00:44:56.450 --> 00:45:00.440
library or to the supermarket.
And what's interesting thing is

00:45:00.440 --> 00:45:04.190
that English marks events as
ongoing, were very action

00:45:04.190 --> 00:45:07.700
oriented in our language. So if
I'm writing a poem when my

00:45:07.700 --> 00:45:11.600
spouse calls and he asks me what
I'm doing, I'll say I'm writing,

00:45:11.690 --> 00:45:14.600
regardless of the fact that I
had stopped to answer the phone.

00:45:15.050 --> 00:45:19.040
German simply doesn't have this
feature. And so it's interesting

00:45:19.040 --> 00:45:22.580
to see the two worldviews these
languages carry, even though

00:45:22.580 --> 00:45:25.880
they have a common root. So what
happens when you have a group of

00:45:25.880 --> 00:45:29.450
people with a language that has
no common root but must operate

00:45:29.450 --> 00:45:33.740
within a completely foreign
framework? Specifically, how do

00:45:33.740 --> 00:45:38.330
indigenous people find success
in a colonized world? Now the

00:45:38.330 --> 00:45:42.350
saying things get lost in
translation rings truer than you

00:45:42.350 --> 00:45:45.920
might imagine, when it comes to
indigenous languages. This is

00:45:45.920 --> 00:45:49.400
Mi'kmaq territory. And many of
you might have heard the word

00:45:49.610 --> 00:45:54.020
pjila'si to mean welcome. That's
what it's used for. But the

00:45:54.020 --> 00:45:58.790
literal translation of the
concept equates to, I'll do my

00:45:58.790 --> 00:46:03.860
best. That's a much more nuanced
and meaningful interaction. In

00:46:03.860 --> 00:46:08.150
this moment, a person to person
contract is being agreed upon.

00:46:08.540 --> 00:46:14.240
Think about that. In this
moment, I will try for you. So,

00:46:14.780 --> 00:46:19.520
language, so language isn't just
something we use to get our

00:46:19.520 --> 00:46:23.330
points across. As I said, it's
woven into who we are as people

00:46:23.330 --> 00:46:27.500
and our notions of a broader
identity. So Robert Thomas, no

00:46:27.500 --> 00:46:31.070
relation wrote about that, that
broader identity, and he called

00:46:31.070 --> 00:46:34.850
it peoplehood, when referring to
indigenous peoples. Now, the

00:46:34.850 --> 00:46:37.490
term peoplehood is a little bit
odd. So I'm going to break it

00:46:37.490 --> 00:46:41.300
down for you, people who have
differs from identity and that

00:46:41.300 --> 00:46:44.660
it not only encompasses the
individual identity, but how

00:46:44.660 --> 00:46:48.260
that person ties it to a larger
collection of peoples and how

00:46:48.260 --> 00:46:50.690
that in turn connects them to
other people within that

00:46:50.690 --> 00:46:54.680
collective. So for example, I
don't speak Mi'kmaq, and I grew

00:46:54.680 --> 00:46:57.920
up off reserve, but I still
identify as a Mi'kmaq person,

00:46:57.920 --> 00:47:01.250
I'm still within the concept of
a Mi'kmaq peoplehood. And that,

00:47:01.250 --> 00:47:05.600
in turn, connects me to somebody
who grew up on reserve, and who

00:47:05.600 --> 00:47:09.230
speaks the language. And it's
getting a bit complex, so please

00:47:09.230 --> 00:47:13.400
bear with me. peoplehood is made
up of four things territory,

00:47:13.400 --> 00:47:18.050
sacred history, ceremonial
cycle, and language, each thread

00:47:18.050 --> 00:47:21.740
informs the other, you can't
address a single aspect of it

00:47:21.740 --> 00:47:25.220
without looking at the whole,
think of a spiderweb. If you

00:47:25.220 --> 00:47:27.980
clip a single thread that
anchors it, the whole thing

00:47:27.980 --> 00:47:32.390
starts to collapse. So the
Mi'kmaq language, for example,

00:47:32.390 --> 00:47:36.140
with its unique characteristics
gives meaning to a sacred

00:47:36.140 --> 00:47:39.770
history, which dictates the use
of the land which is crucial to

00:47:39.770 --> 00:47:44.600
the appropriate execution of a
ceremonial cycle, which in turn,

00:47:44.930 --> 00:47:48.410
is performed in many of the
languages that those ceremonies

00:47:48.410 --> 00:47:53.210
encompass. So firstly, that is a
direct quote from my MA. So I'm

00:47:53.210 --> 00:47:55.550
going to take this moment and
feel vindicated in my decision

00:47:55.550 --> 00:47:59.750
to pursue another arts degree.
And secondly, indigenous peoples

00:48:00.020 --> 00:48:04.820
are complicated and complex.
Imagine centuries ago, when we

00:48:04.820 --> 00:48:07.040
were writing the treaties and
the language we were

00:48:07.040 --> 00:48:10.910
communicating in encompassed,
how we would use the land, how

00:48:10.910 --> 00:48:13.280
would we carry our culture
forward? How would we develop

00:48:13.280 --> 00:48:17.900
relationships with the settlers
and colonizers, now how much of

00:48:17.900 --> 00:48:21.530
that was lost in translation
when they were recorded in

00:48:21.530 --> 00:48:26.120
English, and French. So I
digress. But remember those four

00:48:26.120 --> 00:48:30.650
strands, language, ceremonial
cycle territory, and sacred

00:48:30.650 --> 00:48:34.460
history, the best part about
peoplehood is that it is

00:48:34.460 --> 00:48:39.410
tremendously flexible, we can
swap out and alter some of those

00:48:39.410 --> 00:48:44.540
strands without compromising our
status as indigenous people. So

00:48:44.540 --> 00:48:48.020
once again, I'm going to use my
lived experience as an example,

00:48:48.230 --> 00:48:51.290
you already know, I don't speak
mega mom. And that's because

00:48:51.290 --> 00:48:55.430
through residential school, my
primary language thread has been

00:48:55.430 --> 00:49:00.710
replaced with English and
French. So if I go to a ceremony

00:49:00.710 --> 00:49:03.620
that's conducted entirely in the
megamall language, the

00:49:03.620 --> 00:49:07.490
significance of that ceremony
might not fully resonate with me

00:49:07.490 --> 00:49:10.910
because I want to understand the
full scope of what's being said,

00:49:10.910 --> 00:49:13.850
but that does not diminish who I
am as an Mi'kmaq person.

00:49:14.870 --> 00:49:17.120
Furthermore, there are many
Mi'kmaq people who are

00:49:17.120 --> 00:49:19.970
practicing Catholics. So even
though they smudge and they

00:49:19.970 --> 00:49:23.810
dance, and they drum and they
sweat, they also go to church

00:49:23.810 --> 00:49:26.480
and pray in the megamall
language and that does not take

00:49:26.510 --> 00:49:30.410
away from them they have woven
another set of ceremonies into

00:49:30.410 --> 00:49:35.660
their ceremonial threat. Because
being an indigenous person is a

00:49:35.660 --> 00:49:40.100
lived experience. It's there's
no spectrum whereby somebody is

00:49:40.100 --> 00:49:44.240
more or less native than
somebody else. Any arguments

00:49:44.240 --> 00:49:47.720
over authenticity are us
internalizing a history of

00:49:47.720 --> 00:49:52.730
colonialism that has created
rubrics and tools to measure our

00:49:52.730 --> 00:49:56.630
ethnicity. And blood quantum has
nothing to do with it. The fact

00:49:56.630 --> 00:49:59.630
that my mother is non native
does not take away from the fact

00:49:59.630 --> 00:50:01.100
that I am Mi'kmaq person.

00:50:02.380 --> 00:50:06.310
So with that said, I'm going to
check in with you guys. Language

00:50:06.340 --> 00:50:10.570
shapes your worldview, and is
one of four aspects that create

00:50:10.900 --> 00:50:13.930
an indigenous people who had an
indigenous understanding of

00:50:13.930 --> 00:50:18.340
knowing of how the world works.
So when you look at the picture

00:50:18.370 --> 00:50:21.160
that I'm trying to paint for
you, you're going to understand

00:50:21.160 --> 00:50:25.030
what you're looking at. So
here's the picture. Indigenous

00:50:25.030 --> 00:50:28.570
peoples are the fastest growing
demographic in Canada, our

00:50:28.570 --> 00:50:32.680
median age is 28 years old
compared to that a 43, to the

00:50:32.680 --> 00:50:35.890
non native world. And what that
means is you're going to see

00:50:35.890 --> 00:50:38.860
more of us in your classrooms,
in your workspaces in in your

00:50:38.860 --> 00:50:41.380
public spaces, the world is
going to have to learn to

00:50:41.380 --> 00:50:44.530
interact with us in ways that
are more meaningful and

00:50:44.530 --> 00:50:47.530
significant than what they have
done in the past. I don't need

00:50:47.530 --> 00:50:51.490
to elaborate for you all to
know, we don't have the best

00:50:51.490 --> 00:50:56.860
history. So what? That's the
question I always get asked, how

00:50:56.890 --> 00:51:00.220
does this apply to the real
world, I work in Student

00:51:00.220 --> 00:51:04.060
Services in education, I act as
a go between for the native and

00:51:04.060 --> 00:51:07.540
non native world, I advocate for
students who have a hard time

00:51:07.540 --> 00:51:10.840
speaking up about themselves, I
coach faculty on how to have

00:51:10.840 --> 00:51:14.200
appropriate cultural supports.
And yet, in spite of what I try

00:51:14.200 --> 00:51:18.580
to do, sometimes the effects and
indigenous student feels are too

00:51:18.580 --> 00:51:22.660
great, and they leave to go
home. And it's not that they're

00:51:22.660 --> 00:51:25.690
not smart. It's not that they
aren't capable of doing the

00:51:25.690 --> 00:51:30.880
work. It's just that this world
doesn't get them. Our protocols

00:51:30.880 --> 00:51:35.620
are concepts of time, our
ethics, etc, are just different.

00:51:36.250 --> 00:51:38.710
And it doesn't mean we can't
communicate with your world.

00:51:38.710 --> 00:51:41.260
We've been doing it for the
better part of five centuries.

00:51:41.440 --> 00:51:44.830
It's that we ask that you now
try to communicate with our

00:51:44.830 --> 00:51:48.580
world. And it is at this moment
that I introduced the concept of

00:51:48.610 --> 00:51:53.020
etuaptmumk of two-eyed seeing it
was introduced the elder Albert

00:51:53.020 --> 00:51:57.160
Marshall. Two-eyed seeing tanks
takes the strengths of both a

00:51:57.160 --> 00:52:00.970
colonized world and both an
indigenous world and ask that

00:52:00.970 --> 00:52:05.860
the user see through both lenses
simultaneously to find success.

00:52:06.220 --> 00:52:09.700
It is the magma understanding of
the gift of multiple

00:52:09.700 --> 00:52:15.190
perspectives to see multiple
contexts simultaneously. For

00:52:15.190 --> 00:52:22.720
example, you see Nova Scotia,
Nouvelle-Écosse, Canada, we see

00:52:22.960 --> 00:52:27.700
Mi'kma'ki, traditional
territory, Turtle Island. It's

00:52:27.700 --> 00:52:34.270
not either or it's both at the
same time. Two-eyed seeing was

00:52:34.270 --> 00:52:37.480
originally introduced to build
greater capacity for STEM

00:52:37.480 --> 00:52:39.910
programming for Indigenous
students and post secondary

00:52:39.910 --> 00:52:42.970
institutions, and is now being
adopted nationally by

00:52:42.970 --> 00:52:45.640
organizations and institutions
who are interested in trans

00:52:45.640 --> 00:52:49.510
cultural collaboration two-eyed
seeing implies responsibilities

00:52:49.510 --> 00:52:52.480
for reciprocity, mutual
accountability and co learning.

00:52:52.480 --> 00:52:57.460
It's what we tried to build our
treaties on. We want you to know

00:52:57.460 --> 00:53:01.870
about us and we want success in
this world, but we are no longer

00:53:01.870 --> 00:53:06.370
willing to give up our world to
do it. And you might be asking

00:53:06.370 --> 00:53:10.240
yourself, why would I go through
so much trouble? To indigenize?

00:53:10.240 --> 00:53:13.990
The institution for such a small
number of students in the

00:53:13.990 --> 00:53:19.660
answer, my friends, is
beautiful. It has been shown it

00:53:19.660 --> 00:53:23.200
has been proven that when
institutions implement

00:53:23.200 --> 00:53:27.490
indigenous pedagogy and learning
styles, students across the

00:53:27.490 --> 00:53:33.430
board benefit, native and non
native. Because we recognize the

00:53:33.430 --> 00:53:38.440
multiple contexts and paradigms
that learners learn through our

00:53:38.440 --> 00:53:41.350
ways of teaching have
traditionally been more

00:53:41.350 --> 00:53:43.600
universal, and more inclusive.

00:53:44.680 --> 00:53:45.160
So

00:53:46.380 --> 00:53:50.100
with all of my academic speak,
and concept introduction, I

00:53:50.100 --> 00:53:56.370
bring it back to the elders, who
always tend to say it best with

00:53:56.370 --> 00:54:00.540
regards to education, and
relationship building. According

00:54:00.540 --> 00:54:04.470
to Albert Marshall, the
foundational basis of any

00:54:04.470 --> 00:54:11.100
relationship is an exchange of
stories. So as a storyteller,

00:54:11.520 --> 00:54:17.340
and as a poet, I will leave you
all with what I do best, a poem

00:54:18.360 --> 00:54:26.370
entitled etuaptmumk. I lost my
talk said Rita Jo, for me, I was

00:54:26.370 --> 00:54:29.640
never given the option to know
to feel the flow of the words as

00:54:29.640 --> 00:54:32.640
they rolled off my tongue,
giving me the lyrics and how our

00:54:32.640 --> 00:54:36.270
world was sung. My perspective
was spun using the threads of

00:54:36.270 --> 00:54:39.540
both your world and theirs left
to cobble together a spirit from

00:54:39.540 --> 00:54:43.470
rags and tears painfully aware
that I was different. through

00:54:43.470 --> 00:54:46.650
hard work and determination. I
found my indigenous articulation

00:54:46.650 --> 00:54:51.030
a compilation of two ways makes
up the sum of me. You have two

00:54:51.030 --> 00:54:55.080
eyes that you only have one view
your way is best you would argue

00:54:55.080 --> 00:54:58.740
centuries of being in position
to subdue those who would aspire

00:54:58.740 --> 00:55:02.730
they say the sun never set on
the British Empire. And because

00:55:02.730 --> 00:55:06.510
we recognize the hubris that
defines your story, we have both

00:55:06.510 --> 00:55:11.040
a sunrise and a sunset in our
territory. With my heart and

00:55:11.040 --> 00:55:13.650
eyes, I have a completely
different view, the consequence

00:55:13.650 --> 00:55:17.520
of my skin comes in an entirely
different hue, don't you see,

00:55:17.640 --> 00:55:21.000
although you represent us, we
think very differently than you

00:55:21.000 --> 00:55:23.850
because we see the world not
through one set of eyes. But

00:55:23.850 --> 00:55:28.170
through to thousands of years
long, we were independent, proud

00:55:28.170 --> 00:55:31.470
and strong. We belong to this
earth, the way power belongs to

00:55:31.470 --> 00:55:35.580
money and privilege to birth we
put our communities first. But

00:55:35.580 --> 00:55:39.240
then came the fleets filled with
those you would ironically

00:55:39.240 --> 00:55:43.380
define today as come from a way
to invade every inch of our

00:55:43.380 --> 00:55:46.080
world to break our spirits and
pull the threads that would

00:55:46.110 --> 00:55:49.920
unfurl us to catch the way you
speak. But this is not a poem

00:55:49.920 --> 00:55:54.390
for the retelling of a one sided
history. Each of our worlds has

00:55:54.390 --> 00:55:59.610
their strengths. Yours is in
power. It gets to eat its cake

00:55:59.610 --> 00:56:02.610
and define race. It has the
ability to unapologetically take

00:56:02.640 --> 00:56:06.960
up space. If societal progress
is linear. This society is top

00:56:06.960 --> 00:56:11.640
tier Terra nullius, as though we
were never here, it must be nice

00:56:11.820 --> 00:56:16.830
to be so confident. This society
is ubiquitous built on rarefied

00:56:16.830 --> 00:56:19.590
rubrics of tradition and
rhetoric, your notions of

00:56:19.590 --> 00:56:22.770
diversity are ad hoc in nature
and afterthought feature to an

00:56:22.770 --> 00:56:26.880
immovable structure. But this is
not a conviction, nor an

00:56:26.880 --> 00:56:30.360
acquittal. Just the voice coming
from an eye trained to be

00:56:30.360 --> 00:56:33.300
critical. And if you push the
two sides of our Venn diagram

00:56:33.300 --> 00:56:36.900
together, you'll get our circle.
We were never meant to be

00:56:36.900 --> 00:56:39.540
static. Like the rivers around
us, we shift and change and

00:56:39.540 --> 00:56:41.880
remain dynamic, we bring
something to the table that is

00:56:41.910 --> 00:56:44.610
able to change your worldview
and show you what we are capable

00:56:44.640 --> 00:56:48.720
of that a lot can come from a
holistic concept of the earth,

00:56:48.840 --> 00:56:53.940
you are not a flag, nor are we a
curse or a problem in need of a

00:56:53.940 --> 00:56:56.520
solution. And we've got to rid
ourselves of the spiritual

00:56:56.520 --> 00:57:00.060
resolution, the dilution of our
treaties written to share this

00:57:00.060 --> 00:57:04.410
land. And I asked that you
understand that we are the

00:57:04.410 --> 00:57:08.820
